  <!-- ═══ 9c. WHAT WORKING TOGETHER LOOKS LIKE ═════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="bg-background border-t border-border/25">
    <div class="container mx-auto px-6 py-20 md:py-24 max-w-3xl">

      <h2 class="font-heading text-3xl md:text-4xl text-foreground mb-4">
        What working together looks like
      </h2>
      <p class="text-muted-foreground/65 font-body text-sm leading-relaxed mb-12 max-w-2xl">
        The process adapts to what each young man needs at each stage. This is what it typically looks like.
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-8">

        <div class="border-l-2 border-primary/25 pl-6">
          <p class="font-heading text-sm text-foreground/85 mb-2">Parental consultation</p>
          <p class="font-body text-sm text-muted-foreground/65 leading-relaxed">
            Before your son is involved in anything, you and Michael speak directly. Forty-five minutes to describe what is happening, what you are concerned about, and what you are hoping for. Michael forms a clear picture of the situation and tells you honestly whether this is the right fit, or whether something else would serve better. If you go forward, this conversation shapes how everything begins.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border-l-2 border-primary/25 pl-6">
          <p class="font-heading text-sm text-foreground/85 mb-2">First session</p>
          <p class="font-body text-sm text-muted-foreground/65 leading-relaxed">
            The first session with the young man is a genuine getting-to-know-you. No intake forms, no agenda. A real conversation: what his life is actually like, what matters to him, what is hard, what he notices about himself. The aim is for him to leave feeling heard rather than assessed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border-l-2 border-primary/25 pl-6">
          <p class="font-heading text-sm text-foreground/85 mb-2">First month</p>
          <p class="font-body text-sm text-muted-foreground/65 leading-relaxed">
            Sessions are weekly or fortnightly. This period is about establishing the foundation: what this young man is actually dealing with, what capacities he most needs to build, where the entry points are. Often a simple practice is introduced: something in the breathwork or attention space that can be used between sessions.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border-l-2 border-primary/25 pl-6">
          <p class="font-heading text-sm text-foreground/85 mb-2">Three months in</p>
          <p class="font-body text-sm text-muted-foreground/65 leading-relaxed">
            By this point, meaningful shifts are usually visible. How the young man speaks about himself has changed. He is more articulate about what is happening internally. Regulation is more visible. Decisions are less reactive. Parents often notice a change in how he is in the family before they hear about it from Michael.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border-l-2 border-primary/25 pl-6">
          <p class="font-heading text-sm text-foreground/85 mb-2">Six months and beyond</p>
          <p class="font-body text-sm text-muted-foreground/65 leading-relaxed">
            The deeper changes happen here. The patterns that took years to form take time to genuinely shift. Six months in, sessions are less about introducing new capacities and more about consolidating what has developed, working through what remains unresolved, and building the relationship with self-reflection that carries forward on its own.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="border-l-2 border-primary/10 pl-6">
          <p class="font-body text-sm text-muted-foreground/45 leading-relaxed italic">
            Michael has no interest in creating dependency. The aim is for a young man to finish with a set of internal resources he did not have before: the capacity to regulate, reflect, and orient himself. When that is in place, the sessions are done. Some young men return at transitions, before university or after a difficult period. That is always welcome.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

