  <!-- ═══ 10b. WHO THIS IS NOT THE RIGHT FIT FOR ═══════════════════════ -->
  <section class="bg-background border-t border-border/25">
    <div class="container mx-auto px-6 py-16 md:py-20 max-w-3xl">

      <h2 class="font-heading text-2xl md:text-3xl text-foreground mb-10">
        Who this is not the right fit for
      </h2>

      <div class="space-y-6 font-body text-sm text-muted-foreground/65 leading-relaxed">

        <p>
          This is not the right fit if your son is in acute clinical crisis. If he is at risk of harm to himself or others, experiencing symptoms that need clinical assessment, or has a clinical diagnosis that is currently unstable, he needs clinical care first. Michael will say so directly if he believes that is the case, and will help you find the right referral.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is not the right fit if the primary goal is behavioural compliance. If the aim is for a young man to be more obedient, less disruptive, or easier to manage at home or at school, this is not the right approach. This process supports autonomy and self-direction. It makes young men more themselves, not more manageable.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is not the right fit if you are hoping for fast transformation. The capacities built here take time to form. Families who expect a short intervention producing a changed young man within weeks are likely to be disappointed. Sustained engagement is required on both sides.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is not the right fit if the young man is completely unwilling. A degree of reluctance is normal and workable. Compelled engagement is not. A young man who attends only because he has been made to, and has no interest in being there, will not benefit, and Michael will tell you so.
        </p>

        <p class="text-muted-foreground/45 italic">
          If any of these describe your situation, the parental consultation is still worth having, even if only to think through what the right next step actually is. A conversation that helps you locate the right kind of support is not a wasted conversation.
        </p>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

